  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two capacitors having capacitance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively are connected as shown in figure. Initially, capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is charged to a potential difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> volt by a battery. The battery is then removed and the charged capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is now connected to uncharged capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> by closing the switch <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math>. The amount of charge on the capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, after equilibrium, is</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0069_c7f802acc7d8.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 105px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mfrac><mi>V</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>V</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mi>V</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mi>V</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The initial charge on the capacitor&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>V</mi></math>. If after closing the&nbsp; switch the charge flown through the circuit is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>q</mi></math>, the charge on&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>q</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>q</mi></math>. The potential across the capacitors will be same. Hence,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>V</mi></math></p>"
  }
}